    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi professor, i'm struggling with phonetics. can u explain the technical difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? like, what's actually happening with the vocal cords for the /z/ and /s/ sounds?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The main technical difference lies in vocal cord vibration. For the unvoiced /s/, air passes over a narrow space created by the tongue near the alveolar ridge with no vibration of the vocal folds. Conversely, the voiced /z/ involves the vocal cords vibrating continuously as air forces its way through the same manner of constriction at the alveolar place of articulation. Essentially, /s/ is just voiceless friction, while /z/ is voiced friction.",
